menu "BuddyOS display firmware"

config BUDDY_NET
    bool "Bring up Wi-Fi and MQTT"
    default y
    help
        Off gives the face alone, which is the baseline the memory figures are
        measured against. On is the number the planning docs actually ask for.

config BUDDY_WIFI_SSID
    string "Wi-Fi SSID"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_WIFI_PASS
    string "Wi-Fi password"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_MQTT_URI
    string "MQTT broker URI"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default "mqtt://test.mosquitto.org:1883"
    help
        mqtt:// is plaintext. mqtts:// pulls in mbedTLS, whose handshake is the
        expensive moment — and on this product line a starved handshake has already
        been seen to misreport itself as NOT_TRUSTED rather than as out of memory.

config BUDDY_MQTT_USER
    string "MQTT username (the device serial)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""

config BUDDY_MQTT_PASS
    string "MQTT password (the device JWT)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default ""
    help
        The token ../user-service-viettel mints at POST /v1/mqtt/generate-token. It
        carries the EMQX ACL, so which topics this device may touch is decided by
        the token rather than by anything here.

config BUDDY_MQTT_ENV
    string "Topic namespace (the stem-gateway environment)"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default "staging"
    help
        Topics are {env}/stem/device/{username}/{directive,event,log}. It must match
        the `environment` claim in the token or every publish is denied.

config BUDDY_MQTT_AWS_ROOT
    bool "Verify the broker with the embedded Amazon Root CA 1"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default y
    help
        The product broker sits behind an AWS certificate, and one root is 1.2 KB
        against the full bundle's 65 KB. Turn this off to reach a broker with some
        other publicly-trusted certificate, which costs the whole bundle.

config BUDDY_TLS_PROBE
    bool "Open a second TLS session once MQTT is connected"
    depends on BUDDY_NET
    default n
    help
        A measurement, not a feature. The voice path brings DTLS-SRTP — a second TLS
        context on top of the broker's — and the question that decides whether it
        fits is what the *second* one costs, not the first. This opens one and
        reports the heap on both sides of it.

        Off by default: it dials an outside host, which a shipped device has no
        business doing.

config BUDDY_LIPSYNC_DEMO
    bool "Lip-sync the face to an embedded speech clip"
    default y
    help
        This device has no audio hardware yet, so the only way to see the mouth
        follow real speech is to play a recording through the same classifier the
        product will feed from the decoder. Costs ~75 KB of flash for 2.4 s of
        Vietnamese and English from the shipped Omnivoice voices.

        Off strips the classifier from the image entirely — which is the honest
        baseline, and also the trap: an earlier measurement here looked wonderful
        because the linker had quietly dropped the whole networking stack.

endmenu
